.\" nst-cockpit(8) - cluster-wide user and auth management
.TH NST-COCKPIT 8 "November 2026" "nst-cockpit 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.nh
.SH NAME
nst-cockpit \- manage accounts, SSH, sudo and resource limits on every node of a small Linux cluster from one file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBnst-cockpit grant\fR \fIuser\fR \fItoken\fR... [\fB\-\-on\fR \fInodes\fR]
\fBnst-cockpit revoke\fR \fIuser\fR [\fItoken\fR...] [\fB\-\-on\fR \fInodes\fR]
\fBnst-cockpit lock\fR|\fBunlock\fR \fIuser\fR [\fB\-\-on\fR \fInodes\fR]
\fBnst-cockpit where\fR \fIuser\fR
\fBnst-cockpit passwd\fR \fIuser\fR [\fB\-\-on\fR \fInodes\fR]
\fBnst-cockpit delete\fR \fIuser\fR [\fB\-\-purge\fR]
\fBnst-cockpit adopt\fR \fIuser\fR
\fBnst-cockpit prune\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
\fBnst-cockpit init\fR \fIcontrol-node\fR [\fInode\fR...]
\fBnst-cockpit sync\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fInode\fR...]
\fBnst-cockpit audit\fR [\fInode\fR...]
\fBnst-cockpit status\fR [\fInode\fR...]
\fBnst-cockpit list\fR | \fBroles\fR | \fBversion\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nst-cockpit
keeps every node of a cluster in step with one source of truth on a
.IR "control node" .
Each user is one line in
.IR access.conf .
Each line lists the user's roles and flags.
.B sync
pushes that state to every node over SSH.
.PP
On each node, sync does the following:
.IP \(bu 2
creates missing accounts, using the user's UID from the control node when it is free;
.IP \(bu 2
sets the membership of every role group to exactly what
.I access.conf
says, removing anyone who is not listed;
.IP \(bu 2
locks or unlocks accounts;
.IP \(bu 2
writes its own sshd, sudoers and pam_limits files;
.IP \(bu 2
writes per-user systemd slice limits and applies disk quotas.
.PP
Accounts that are not listed are never modified. They are reported as UNMANAGED.
.PP
Run it on the control node as your own user, not as root.
It reaches the other nodes with your SSH key and uses
.B sudo \-n
there, so your account needs passwordless sudo on every node except the control node.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Debian or Ubuntu nodes, with systemd.
.IP \(bu 2
OpenSSH with
.B Include /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf
at the top of
.IR sshd_config ,
which is the default on Ubuntu 20.04 and later and on Debian 11 and later.
.IP \(bu 2
sudo with
.B @includedir /etc/sudoers.d
or
.BR #includedir .
.IP \(bu 2
bash 4.4 or later, awk (mawk or gawk), openssl, iproute2.
.IP \(bu 2
Optional: the
.B quota
package, for the
.B quota
role key.
.SH ACCESS.CONF
One user per line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\fIuser\fR  \fItoken\fR [\fItoken\fR ...]   [# comment]
.RE
.fi
.PP
A
.I token
is a role or a flag. Either can be scoped to nodes with
.BR @ :
.TP
.I role
The role on its default nodes, as set in
.IR roles.ini .
.TP
.IB role @ node , node
The role on the listed nodes only.
.TP
.B locked
The account is locked (password locked and account expired, so SSH keys are blocked too), and its running processes are killed.
.TP
.B nopasswd
Passwordless sudo
.RB ( "NOPASSWD: ALL" )
on every node where the user also has sudo through a role.
.TP
.B pwonly
Password-only SSH: public keys are refused.
.PP
Flags can be scoped like roles, for example
.BR locked@gpu1 .
A user with no role must be marked
.BR locked .
In commands, node names may be shortened when the result is unambiguous
.RB ( 3
for
.BR node3 );
they are always stored in full.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
alice      admin nopasswd
bob        admin
carol      student
dave       student gpu pwonly@node1
erin       user@node3,node4
frank      locked
.RE
.fi
.SH ROLES.INI
One
.BI [ name ]
section per role.
.B nst-cockpit roles
prints the effective definitions.
.TP
.B groups
Unix groups the role puts users in, comma separated. Required.
Every group named in
.I roles.ini
is managed exactly on every node.
.TP
.B nodes
Default nodes:
.B all
or a comma list. A token
.IB role @ nodes
overrides it.
Default:
.BR all .
.TP
.B password
.B yes
allows SSH password login for these groups.
Default:
.BR no .
.TP
.B from
.BR any ,
.BR control ,
or a comma list of source IPs.
SSH for these groups is accepted only from those addresses, unless the user also has a role with
.BR "from = any" .
.B control
means the addresses of the control node's default-route interface. This gives the jump-host pattern: users must log in to the control node first.
Default:
.BR any .
.TP
.B sshd
Extra sshd options for these groups, as
.IR Key = Val , Key = Val .
.TP
.B sudo
.B yes
writes
.B "%group ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL"
for each group of the role.
Not needed for roles that include the
.B SUDO_GROUP
group.
Default:
.BR no .
.TP
.B home
Base directory for accounts that sync creates through this role.
Existing homes are never moved.
Default:
.IR /home .
.TP
.B slice
Per-user systemd slice limits (for example
.BR CPUQuota=800%,MemoryMax=20G ),
written to
.IR /etc/systemd/system/user\- UID .slice.d/50\-nst\-limits.conf .
.TP
.B quota
Block quota per user (for example
.BR 90G )
on the filesystem that holds
.BR home .
It is only applied when that path is a quota-enabled mount.
.TP
.B limits
pam_limits for the group, as
.IR item = value , item = value ,
written to
.IR /etc/security/limits.d/60\-nst\-cockpit.conf .
.PP
The default
.I roles.ini
defines
.B admin
(groups
.BR cluster\-admins ", " sudo :
SSH and sudo everywhere) and
.B user
(group
.BR cluster\-users :
plain SSH).
It also contains commented examples for a
.B student
role and a
.B gpu
role.
.SH COCKPIT.CONF
Optional site settings, in shell syntax:
.TP
.B CONTROL
Name of the control node, as written in
.IR nodes .
Default: the first line of
.IR nodes .
.TP
.B SUDO_GROUP
The group that the distribution's
.I /etc/sudoers
grants full sudo. Default:
.BR sudo .
.TP
.B SHELL_ROLE
The role
.B init
uses for SSH access it finds outside other roles. Default:
.BR user .
.TP
.BR LEGACY_SSHD ", " LEGACY_SUDO ", " LEGACY_LIMITS
Space-separated file names in
.IR sshd_config.d ,
.I sudoers.d
and
.I limits.d
that nst-cockpit replaces.
They are removed only after the new files validate.
Use these to absorb an existing hand-made setup.
.TP
.B LEGACY_SLICE_MARKER
Regular expression for slice drop-ins written by another tool that nst-cockpit may take over or remove.
.TP
.B LEGACY_MANAGERS
Names of old management scripts;
.B audit
warns while they are still installed.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands marked
.I (syncs)
edit
.I access.conf
and then run
.B sync
on all nodes.
.TP
\fBgrant\fR \fIuser\fR \fItoken\fR... [\fB\-\-on\fR \fInodes\fR]
Add roles or flags; at least one token is required.
A scope is merged with any existing one.
Creates the line if the user is new.
.I (syncs)
.TP
\fBrevoke\fR \fIuser\fR [\fItoken\fR...] [\fB\-\-on\fR \fInodes\fR]
Remove roles or flags.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-\-on ,
removes them only on those nodes.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.B \-\-on
and no tokens, removes everything the user has on those nodes.
.IP \(bu 2
With neither, drops the user from
.IR access.conf .
The accounts stay; see
.B prune
and
.BR delete .
.RE
.I (syncs)
.TP
\fBlock\fR|\fBunlock\fR \fIuser\fR [\fB\-\-on\fR \fInodes\fR]
Set or clear the
.B locked
flag.
If the user is not in
.IR access.conf ,
the lock is applied directly on the nodes instead.
.I (syncs)
.TP
\fBwhere\fR \fIuser\fR
For every node, shows:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
UID, and whether the account is locked;
.IP \(bu 2
password state (set, unset, or must change);
.IP \(bu 2
SSH key count, open sessions, last login;
.IP \(bu 2
home, quota and groups.
.RE
.TP
\fBpasswd\fR \fIuser\fR [\fB\-\-on\fR \fInodes\fR]
Sets one random temporary password on the given nodes (default: all) and forces a change at the next login.
The password is printed once.
.TP
\fBdelete\fR \fIuser\fR [\fB\-\-purge\fR]
Deletes the account on every node, after you type the username to confirm.
It kills the user's processes, removes the crontab and the line in
.IR access.conf .
.B \-\-purge
also removes the home directory, slice limits and quota record.
.TP
\fBadopt\fR \fIuser\fR
Builds an
.I access.conf
line from the user's current state on all nodes, shows it, and appends it after confirmation.
.TP
\fBprune\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Locks accounts that exist on a node where the user has no access.
.TP
\fBinit\fR \fIcontrol-node\fR [\fInode\fR...]
One-time setup, run on the control node. Writes
.IR nodes ,
.IR cockpit.conf ,
.I roles.ini
and
.IR sshd-baseline ,
then inventories every node and drafts
.IR access.conf .
Changes nothing on the nodes.
.TP
\fBsync\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fInode\fR...]
Makes the nodes match
.IR access.conf ,
control node first.
.B \-\-dry\-run
shows:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
SSH gains and losses;
.IP \(bu 2
every account, group and lock change;
.IP \(bu 2
file diffs;
.IP \(bu 2
the content of every legacy file it would remove.
.RE
.TP
\fBaudit\fR [\fInode\fR...]
Read-only report of every auth-related setting:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
effective sshd settings;
.IP \(bu 2
sshd, sudoers, limits and slice files nst-cockpit does not own;
.IP \(bu 2
extra lines in the main
.IR /etc/sudoers ;
.IP \(bu 2
PAM gaps;
.IP \(bu 2
UID 0 accounts and empty passwords;
.IP \(bu 2
non-root-owned binaries in root's PATH;
.IP \(bu 2
legacy managers and unmanaged accounts;
.IP \(bu 2
UIDs that differ across nodes.
.RE
.TP
\fBstatus\fR [\fInode\fR...]
Role group members and owned files per node.
.TP
.BR list ", " roles ", " version
Print
.IR access.conf ,
the effective role table, or the version.
.SH ADOPTING AN EXISTING CLUSTER
.IP 1. 4
Snapshot
.IR /etc/passwd ,
.IR /etc/shadow ,
.IR /etc/group ,
.IR /etc/gshadow ,
.IR /etc/ssh ,
.I /etc/sudoers*
and
.I /etc/security
on every node.
.IP 2. 4
On the control node:
.BR "nst-cockpit init" " \fIcontrol-node node\fR..."
It writes the configuration and drafts
.I access.conf
from what it finds. Nothing on the nodes changes.
.IP 3. 4
Run
.B "nst-cockpit audit"
and read every WARN line.
.IP 4. 4
Edit
.I roles.ini
to match the groups you already use, and list the old files in the
.B LEGACY_*
settings of
.IR cockpit.conf .
If you changed roles, redraft with
.BR "sudo rm /etc/nst-cockpit/access.conf && nst-cockpit init" .
.IP 5. 4
Resolve every
.B review
in
.IR access.conf .
.IP 6. 4
Run
.B "nst-cockpit sync \-\-dry\-run"
until it shows no REFUSING and every SSH loss is intended.
.IP 7. 4
Sync one node at a time, control node last, keeping a root shell open:
.BR "nst-cockpit sync node3" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/nst-cockpit/
Configuration on the control node:
.IR access.conf ,
.IR roles.ini ,
.IR nodes ,
.IR cockpit.conf ,
.IR sshd-baseline .
Override the directory with
.BR NST_COCKPIT_CONF .
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/10\-nst\-cockpit.conf
Baseline settings plus
.BR AllowGroups .
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/99\-nst\-cockpit.conf
Match blocks: password login, per-group options, source restrictions,
.BR pwonly .
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/10\-nst\-cockpit
The
.B SUDO_GROUP
rule, role
.B "%group"
rules and NOPASSWD lines.
.TP
.I /etc/security/limits.d/60\-nst\-cockpit.conf
Group pam_limits.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/user\-UID.slice.d/50\-nst\-limits.conf
Per-user cgroup limits.
.TP
.I /var/lib/nst\-cockpit/quota
Record of applied quotas.
.TP
.I /root/nst\-cockpit\-backup/TIMESTAMP/
Copy of
.IR sshd_config.d ,
.I sudoers.d
and
.I limits.d
taken before every change.
.PP
Do not edit owned files by hand: the next sync overwrites them.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
Every node refuses the sync if it would remove the operator from
.BR SUDO_GROUP ,
remove the operator's NOPASSWD on nodes other than the control node, or leave
.B SUDO_GROUP
without members.
.IP \(bu 2
Sudoers changes are applied in two steps. The new file is validated with
.BR "visudo \-cf" ;
the legacy files are then removed and the whole tree is checked with
.BR "visudo \-c" .
Any failure restores the backup.
.IP \(bu 2
sshd changes are checked with
.B "sshd \-t"
before reload. A failure restores the backup.
.IP \(bu 2
Reloading sshd does not end existing sessions.
.IP \(bu 2
Group membership and accounts are not part of the backup. Restore those from your own snapshot.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Set up a cluster (on the control node):
.nf
.B nst-cockpit init node1 node2 node3 gpu1
.fi
.TP
New student on the control node:
.B nst-cockpit grant rahul student
.TP
New admin:
.B nst-cockpit grant priya admin nopasswd
.TP
Shell on one node only:
.B nst-cockpit grant rahul user \-\-on node3
.TP
Remove someone's access on one node:
.B nst-cockpit revoke rahul \-\-on node3
.TP
Suspend, then restore:
.B nst-cockpit lock rahul
;
.B nst-cockpit unlock rahul
.TP
Forgotten password:
.B nst-cockpit passwd rahul
.TP
Drift check (expect no output):
.nf
.B "nst-cockpit sync \-\-dry\-run | grep \-E 'would|REFUSING|UNMANAGED|STRAY'"
.fi
.TP
Bring a node that was offline back in line:
.B nst-cockpit sync node2
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
sudo applies the
.I last
matching rule. A
.B "%group ALL"
line read after a user's NOPASSWD line overrides it.
nst-cockpit writes NOPASSWD lines last. List old sudoers files that grant group rules in
.BR LEGACY_SUDO .
.IP \(bu 2
.B visudo \-c
fails on any sudoers file that is not mode 0440, even an old one. Fix modes, or list the file in
.BR LEGACY_SUDO .
.IP \(bu 2
Group changes take effect at the next login. A session that is already open keeps its old groups.
.IP \(bu 2
The first login after
.B passwd
or account creation must be from an interactive terminal, because the forced password change needs a TTY.
Editor remote extensions fail until the password has been changed once.
.IP \(bu 2
Temporary passwords are generated per run.
Accounts created by separate
.B sync
runs get different passwords; use
.B passwd
to set one password across nodes.
.IP \(bu 2
.B sync
exits 1 when a node is unreachable. The other nodes are still synced.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Validation failed, a node refused, or a node was unreachable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sshd_config (5),
.BR sudoers (5),
.BR limits.conf (5),
.BR systemd.resource-control (5),
.BR setquota (8),
.BR usermod (8)
